16:10:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: You’ve hit on a key difference between the color we see in the physical world and the color on a screen. The way light interacts with objects (reflection/absorption) is **subtractive color mixing**, while a screen uses **additive color mixing**. Let’s break it down. --- ### 1. What you already know: reflective color (subtractive) When white light (which contains all colors of the rainbow) hits a painted wall, an apple, or a printed page: - The material **absorbs** (subtracts) some wavelengths of light and **reflects** the rest. - For example, a red apple reflects mostly red light and absorbs other colors. That reflected light enters your eye, and you see “red.” - If you mix red and green paint, you get a muddy brown/black because together they absorb almost all wavelengths—this is subtractive mixing. --- ### 2. How a screen works: emitting light directly (additive) A computer or TV screen doesn’t rely on reflecting light. Instead, it **emits its own light**. It’s made of millions of tiny dots called **pixels**, and each pixel is really a cluster of three even tinier light sources: - A **red** subpixel - A **green** subpixel - A **blue** subpixel These are the three primary colors of light. By turning them on at different brightness levels, the screen can create any color. - **All off** → black (no light) - **All on at full brightness** → white (all wavelengths combined) - **Red + Green** → yellow - **Red + Blue** → magenta - **Green + Blue** → cyan - **Red at 50%, Green at 10%, Blue at 90%** → a specific shade of purple, etc. This is **additive mixing**: you start with darkness and add light together. The more you add, the closer you get to white. --- ### 3. Why your eye blends the tiny dots The subpixels are so small (far smaller than a human hair) that from a normal viewing distance, you can’t see them individually. Your eye merges the red, green, and blue light into one perceived color. It’s the same principle as pointillist painting, but with light instead of paint. --- ### 4. Key contrast | **Physical objects (subtractive)** | **Screens (additive)** | |------------------------------------------|----------------------------------| | Need external light source | Produce their own light | | Mixing pigments subtracts wavelengths | Mixing light adds wavelengths | | Primary colors: cyan, magenta, yellow | Primary colors: red, green, blue | | All colors mixed → black (muddy) | All colors mixed → white | So in short, a computer screen doesn’t “reflect” colors like a physical object—it shines colored light directly into your eyes, and your brain blends the tiny RGB dots into a full-color picture.
